        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Can you use the NordicTrack T 6.5 S without iFit?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. It runs in manual mode with speed and incline set by hand, and the onboard programmes are available without an active membership. What you lose is the reason to choose the brand — the trainer-led sessions and automatic adjustment. Bought without a subscription it is an ordinary entry-level treadmill with a small screen, and better hardware is available at the money.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the NordicTrack T 6.5 S good for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not for regular running. The published 20 by 55-inch deck is short for anyone over about 5'8" once stride length extends, and the 10 mph ceiling leaves little headroom. It handles walking, incline walking and light jogging well. If running is the goal, deck length is the specification to buy and 60 inches is the target.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>NordicTrack T 6.5 S vs Horizon T101 — which is better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On hardware they are close, and the Horizon generally wins on warranty. The real difference is philosophy: the T101 has no subscription attached and publishes a lifetime frame and motor warranty, while the T 6.5 S is the cheapest way into iFit. Choose on whether guided content is what will get you onto the machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the screen size on the NordicTrack T 6.5 S?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Five inches on the standard model, and this is where the closely named variants cause trouble — related models in the same family ship larger displays. Since the whole proposition is trainer-led content designed for a much bigger screen, confirm the display size against the exact model number you are ordering, and plan on mirroring to a tablet or television.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What weight capacity does the NordicTrack T 6.5 S have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              NordicTrack publishes 300 lb. Apply the body-weight-plus-25% rule, because a running footfall puts several times static weight through the deck — that puts comfortable use at roughly 240 lb and below. Capacity is a proxy for frame rigidity as much as a limit, which is why a heavier user feels the difference as instability rather than as a warning.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the NordicTrack T 6.5 S worth it at full price?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Rarely. It is discounted as a standing strategy, the outgoing model year is cleared regularly, and bundled membership terms move the effective price around considerably. Buying at list is the avoidable mistake with this machine — check what it has actually been selling for over recent months before accepting any "was" price.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

